> Write a short story about a lighthouse keeper who finds a message in a bottle.
The fog had been thick for three days, a suffocating blanket of gray that swallowed the horizon and turned the world into a muffled, damp void. Elias, the keeper of the Blackwood Point Light, preferred it this way. The fog meant fewer ships, fewer questions, and fewer memories of the life he had left behind on the mainland.

He spent his evenings polishing the brass lens, the rhythmic *swish-swish* of the cloth against the glass serving as his only companion. It was during one of these quiet rituals, just as the sun began to bleed through the mist, that he saw it.

It wasn’t a ship. It was a small, dark shape bobbing in the churning surf below the cliff’s edge, caught in the eddy of the tide. Curiosity, a rare and dangerous emotion for a man who had sworn off attachment, got the better of him. He grabbed his oilskin coat and descended the winding stone stairs to the rocky beach.

The object was a bottle, green glass, sealed with wax that had cracked under the pressure of the sea. Inside, a rolled piece of parchment lay curled like a sleeping snake.

Elias carried it back to the lighthouse, his boots heavy with seawater. He placed it on the workbench, the only light coming from the rotating beam above and the small kerosene lamp on the table. With trembling hands, he broke the wax seal. The cork popped with a soft sigh, releasing a scent of brine and old paper.

He unrolled the parchment. The handwriting was shaky, the ink faded to a rusty brown, but legible.

*To whoever finds this,*

*If you are reading this, I am gone. The sea took my ship, the ‘Marigold,’ on the night of the storm in 1924. I was the captain. I loved a woman named Clara, who lived in the house at the end of the pier. I promised I would return before the first snow. I failed.*

*This bottle is not a plea for rescue. It is a confession. I did not die at sea. I swam to shore. I walked to the house. I watched her light burn for three nights, waiting for me. I was too ashamed to knock. I was too afraid of what I had become—a man who had lost his way, his ship, and his honor. So I walked back into the water. I let the tide take me.*

*If you are the keeper of this light, know that I watched your tower every night. Your light was the only thing that kept me from madness in the dark. Thank you for keeping it burning. It was the only promise I kept.*

*— Arthur Penhaligon*

Elias sat back, the paper trembling in his hands. He looked out the window. The fog was lifting, revealing the jagged coastline. He looked toward the end of the pier, where the old, abandoned house stood, its windows dark and broken.

He had always wondered why the light was so important to him. Why he stayed, year after year, in this lonely place. He had thought it was penance for his own failures. But now, he realized it was something else. It was a beacon for the lost, even those who had already found their peace.

Elias picked up his logbook. He didn’t write about the weather or the tide. He wrote a single line:

*The light is for Arthur. And for Clara. And for anyone who is still waiting.*

He climbed the stairs to the lantern room. The beam swept out into the clearing night, cutting through the last wisps of fog, steady and bright, a promise kept across a century of silence.

